[GER_AFR.FullName]
The Last Offensive 
[GER_AFR.Description]

[GER_AFR.Epilogue]
After accumulating forces for half a year and gaining 6-fold supremacy in artillery, tanks and aviation, the US task force, supported by the 8th British Army, threw the Italo-German troops back to the extreme end of the Cape Bon Peninsula, cutting them off the mainland in the process. After two months of fighting, the Afrika Korps was surrounded from both the land and the sea, and laid down arms … Field marshal Rommel was recalled from Tunisia on March 9, 1943 to be appointed commander of the Army Group B in Northern Italy… Major Erich Gerhard fought in Normandy. After failure of Operation “Watch on the Rhine” he was assigned to the Berlin garrison. He died heroically in April 1945 defending Berlin.

‘It is Man that can make the Way great, and not the Way that can make Man great’ Confucius

[Faidpass.FullName]
Beyond the Faid Pass
[Faidpass.Description]
\t Major Erich Gerhard joined the war as a lieutenant in France back in 1940. He was very successful leading a light tank company, for which his men were awarded and Erich himself promoted to captain. Then he fought on the Eastern Front, where he became a Major and was put in charge of a medium tank battalion. In 1942, his battalion was included into the 10th Panzer Division and redeployed to the African theater of war under Rommel.
\t Major Gerhard was reputed to be a caring and valiant commander. Always on the front line, he had a lot of close shaves. In ever-changing battle situations Major Gerhard was capable of making the right decision quickly, and so Rommel assigned his battalion to the most critical sectors of the front, issuing orders directly to the major.
\t On February 14, 1943, Gerhard's tank battalion was expanded by adding more infantry and the 501st Tiger Battalion. Thus, the Major was put in charge of a combat group (Kampfgruppe) and ordered to attack the Faid pass occupied by U.S. forces. Then, acting on his own discretion, he was to join the units of the 21st Panzer Division advancing from the south, thus surrounding the enemy near Sidi Bou Zid. Here is the situation report for February 14, 1943, dispatched to all unit commanders taking part in the offensive:
\t "Operation Spring Breeze (Fruehlingswind).
\t Primary task: make a sudden attack on the U.S. forces concentrated in an area west of the Faid pass to establish a springboard for a further offensive on Eisenhower's troops and to prevent their invasion of Tunisia.
\t Military strength: 10th and 21st Panzer Divisions. The 21st Division attacks from the south while the 10th Division manoeuvres around the enemy from the north. By Sidi Bou Zid, the encirclement must be closed to eliminate the surrounded enemy forces.
\t Enemy strength: 168th Infantry Regiment reinforced with anti-tank guns and tanks.
\t Zero hour: reach the offensive objective by 04:00 on February 14, 1943. By the end of February 14, close the encirclement and eliminate the enemy defences."
\t Gerhard's combat group was ordered to spearhead the forces and attack U.S. defensive positions from the north with infantry and tanks. The order was radioed the night before directly by Rommel.
\t Rommel: "Good evening Major, I have great expectations for your group. You will be the main axis of advance and the first to tear into the town to gain  victory. Air intelligence data indicates a concentration of anti-tank guns to the north. But you will have to advance quickly, so the enemy has no time to pull in reinforcements. May God and the Great German Reich be with you!"
\t Gerhard: "Yes, General, sir! We'll do the utmost of our power!"


[InDefend.FullName]
The Battle of Sidi Bou Zid
[InDefend.Description]
\t The next day, February 15, 1943, was marked by an attempt of U.S. forces to counter-attack and win back the positions they had lost. German command was prepared for that. The plan was simple: to lure the enemy into a trap, use the terrain to surround and eliminate him. Gerhard's group, distinguished on February 14, was chosen to perform the task. The Major decided to leave his artillery and infantry in a small town along the axis of the U.S. forces advance, and use tanks to outflank and attack the enemy. He chose not to use the heavy Tiger tanks due to their bad maneuverability, even though intelligence data indicated that the U.S. forces were to introduce their new Sherman tanks. The Major decided to have his Tigers in reserve for an emergency. 
\t In the evening of February 14, Erich Gerhard received the sad message that his younger brother was killed on the Eastern Front. It was a severe shock, because his "little Jurgen" was not just his blood brother, but also a close friend since the earliest childhood. They were twins with an age difference of thirty minutes. And whatever happened to one was felt by the other. Now Erich felt the most severe loneliness. Still, Major Gerhard was a true German soldier. In the morning of February 15 he led his group to the battle.


[Sbeitla.FullName]
The Battle of Sbeitla
[Sbeitla.Description]
\t Having successfully repelled the enemy counter-attack on February 15, Rommel planned to immediately advance to the town of Sbeitla. This was dictated by the disorder among the U.S. forces and insignificant casualties in the German army. Competent leadership by the subordinate unit commanders had helped in overcoming the enemy - larger in numbers but inexperienced - shedding hardly any German blood. But disagreements within the German high command and hesitation by von Arnim and Ziegle, who demanded confirmations of the continued offensive from Kesselring, spoiled Rommel's plans. The entire day of February 16 was spent with German troops in waiting, which allowed the Americans to reorganize and establish defences. Only by the night of February 16/17 the German high command agreed to continue the offensive. 
\t The 10th Panzer Division, including Gerhard's group, spent the entire day of February 16 hunting the XIX Corps of general Koeltz that had already withdrawn to Sbiba. Thus, it turned out the Division was chasing ghosts around Fondouk and Pichon. Nothing is as frustrating as chase and wait. For that reason, by the start of the offensive, the 10th Panzer Division hadn't been fully prepared. Major Gerhard worked throughout the night, issuing the necessary orders and keeping in touch with headquarters for coordination and further preparation for the operation. Only by the morning of February 17 the group was ready to attack. The loss of a close relative and weariness can demoralize even the strongest of men. During such a time one needs something to rely on. For Major Gerhard it was his father that once said, "Son, life is a battle, in which a weak man will perish, an indifferent man will desert and a strong man will fight."
\t Right before the offensive, Rommel himself visited the troops. He understood that the men were weary and needed rest, but he also knew that his personal example could help to maintain their morale. Having saluted the troops, he had a brief conversation with Major Gerhard.
\t Rommel: "I do count on you and your men, Major. I understand that the men are weary, but the situation demands that we mobilize our best qualities. Such days give rise to heroes to be remembered by generations to come."
\t Gerhard: "General, sir! No need to say this to me and my men. We are soldiers of the German Reich, and we always remember this! However, your presence here, on the front line, will help us forget the weariness and fight valiantly!"


[Kasserine.FullName]
The Kasserine Pass
[Kasserine.Description]
\t The discord in the Italian-German high command continued. Despite the recent success confirming that Rommel was right, he was still disbelieved. His plan to press on the offensive to the northwest via Tebessa was rejected by the German high command. Then Rommel offered the same plan to the Italian headquarters which supported it but preferred an advance to Thala and Le Kef to the north, instead of the northwest. Rommel considered this a lack of foresight, as the key was to cut off the Allies from their primary supply lines. This would force the British and Americans to withdraw to Algeria. The General believed that an offensive to the north would allow the enemy to pull in strong reserves. 
\t The offensive plan had to be revised quickly. This took some time, allowing the Allies to pull in reserves and establish defences. And again valuable time was lost. The 21st and 10th Panzer Divisions were passed on to Rommel, but von Arnim didn't let go of the battalion of heavy Tigers that the General had counted on. 
\t Gerhard's group, a part of the 10th Panzer Division, was also passed on to Rommel. Two days of delay allowed the troops to rest and recover. Despite the recent successes, the group had lost nearly a quarter of personnel and there were little to no reserves. The lack of fresh manpower in the German army as well as discord in the high command prevented the necessary rotation of units. Tension on the front didn't let Erich Gerhard leave the Division for even a few days. He longed for visiting home in Berlin and supporting his mother that had lost a son. He had no time to write her a letter since he had received the message of his brother's death. And now this became possible. His heart was wrung when he thought what his mother was feeling. In 1939, he and his brother were by her when they got terrible news of their father's death in Poland. That time they were there to support her. And now she was facing the tragedy all alone. How could Erich console his mother by a letter. He wrote that he would return no matter what as soon as the war was over. That the deaths were not in vain and that victory will be gained. Major Gerhard believed in victory, in the greatness of Germany and its people. This gave him strength and endurance. He was not a national socialistic fanatic and didn't welcome the policy of atrocity towards enemy. He welcomed the gentleman's attitude to prisoners propagated by German high command in Africa. Once on a briefing Rommel cited the French novelist Alphonse Daudet: "Hate is wrath of the weak". And Gerhard considered the German nation strong.
\t In the evening of February 19, all unit commanders held a meeting in the headquarters of Rommel, who outlined the operation tasks and plan. Gerhard's combat group was ordered to break through the Kasserine pass in the direction of Thala. Rommel underlined that the enemy had amassed considerable forces on that sector of the front, having pulled in reserves, making this a difficult task. 
\t The battle began on February 20 and lasted until the evening.

[Thala.FullName]
On the Way to Thala
[Thala.Description]
\t Having secured the pass, German forces continued the advance to Thala. The 10th Panzer Division advanced with varied success, pushing against the British 26th Armoured Brigade. They fought for every hill. During the battle, whenever the advance slowed down, Rommel personally rushed into the thick of things, forcing 10th Panzer Division commander von Broich to truck infantry to keep up with the tanks. By the end of February 20, German forces met fierce resistance by the British defenders in front of Thala. Having failed to achieve a breakthrough, they had to withdraw to regroup and resume the offensive in the morning of February 21 to finally break down the  enemy defences. 
\t In the evening of February 20, Field Marshal Rommel personally decorated Erich Gerhard, armoured group commander 10th Panzer Division with the Knight's Cross of the Iron Cross award for service in battle and personal courage. Awards of this kind were usually presented with much solemnity in Berlin, often by Adolf Hitler himself. But this time Rommel didn't want his best commander to leave the front, because the situation was tense. Of course, he could postpone awarding to better days, but who knew what would happen the next day.

[Tebessa.FullName]
Tebessa
[Tebessa.Description]
\t The Afrika Korps advanced slower than Rommel expected for several reasons. The first and foremost was the hesitation of the high command, von Arnim and Kesselring in particular. It resulted in long delays that allowed the enemy to pull in reserves and establish defences. Besides, Rommel had to change the axis of advance, as ordered by the Italian high command, which required time to redraw the plans for a new battle scenario. The second reason was the lack of troops. Though Hitler kept his promise and redeployed the 10th and 21st Panzer Divisions to the continent, this was not enough, because the enemy had a lot more manpower to draw from.
\t Having launched the assault on Thala, Rommel had to prevent an imminent flanking maneuver from the west. He sent combat groups commanded by general Bulowius to the west with the order to attack the enemy by Tebessa and secure the town. But nobody expected the enemy to be this strong. On top of that, the dull weather changed, the sun came out, allowing Allied air forces to put on the heat.
\t Gerhard's group joined the forces assaulting Tebessa. By that time its personnel was nearly halved. But the morale of the survivors was high, because they had had a successful campaign so far. Major himself was so occupied with preparations to the battle that all sorrows and joys of the previous days receded into the background. He was in the thick of things and that invigorated him, like a young but experienced wolf before a good hunt. Gerhard felt that the coming battle would determine the future of the operation. He learned much during his service under Rommel. Gerhard both considered the situation in this sector and tried to understand the operational plan in general, as the Field Marshal had devised it.
